A||||7||Bautsch||Removing 88' 4 cyl?||||
Z||000000||Bautsch||09-08-1999||11:04 PM||||I am getting ready to pull my engine in my 88 4 cyl. I understand most of the procedure but I don't have access to a lift. How do I pull the engine out from under the car after I drop it? The only way I can see is to lift the rear and tilt it forward on the front wheels and roll the engine out. Please Help.<P><BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>mike@macvend.com||||reg||
Z||000001||DJRice||09-09-1999||08:42 AM||||You dont necesarily need a lift.  I have done it several times with 2 floor jacks, some concrete blocks, and a couple of pieces of wood.  Basically, you raise the car up.  Disconnect all of the wiring and fuel line, tranny cables etc.  Set the car back down, but with some kind of a dolly or heavy duty crawler (something low with wheels on it) under the cradle.  Disconnect the brakes and associated equipment.  Then raise the car back up and the cradle will stay on the ground.  Make sure to block front wheels.<P>Just take your time and be careful.||||reg||
Z||000002||David||09-10-1999||09:06 PM||||I've taken my '86 4 cyl out from the top twice.  I think that it is easier than dropping the cradle, especially with the limited resources that I have at home.  I have to strip the block of all accessories, except for the distributor and intake system.<P>David||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-10-1999||09:22 PM||||David is right!!! the easiest way to pull the engine out is on the top....||||reg||14
Z||000004||batboy||09-11-1999||12:06 PM||||If you drop the cradle out the bottom, pulling the engine out the side with a rear tire removed might save you from having to lift up the rear of the car so high.<P>I'm going to try pulling my 2.5 out of the top of my 87 SE soon. I might photo document the event. I need to be able to roll and move the car after the engine is out. There are advantages and disadvantages to both ways.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||09-11-1999||06:10 PM||||When I put the V6 in my 88 Coupe, I dropped the cradle on the 88 and the 86 donor. I got it all back together and found out the clutch wasn't working. I then had to drop it again. In all I dropped 3 cradles in the span of 1 week. The last time I did it I got it out in 2 hours. I don't know about going from the top, but I do know that when the cradle is on the ground everything is easy to get at.||||reg||12
Z||000006||fastonpumpgas||09-12-1999||05:02 AM||||I am not saying this is a good way, but once I got it on the ground, me and a few guys just picked up the car and rolled it over the engine, took the back whels of fthat got it lower and freed up the struts, they are no the heavy with nothing in the back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000007||batboy||09-12-1999||12:11 PM||||The below listed link is from V8 Archie's website describing his method of Fiero engine removal.<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch8.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch8.htm</A> <P>Hey, I finally figured out how to create links on the Forum...cool!||||reg||6
